<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Barack Obama</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">b. <a href="/years/1961">1961</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Barack Obama is an American politician who was the 44th president of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> from 2009 to 2017, the first African American to hold the office. His presidency saw the response to the 2008 financial crisis, the Affordable Care Act and a 'pivot' of US policy towards Asia. During a visit to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vietnam">Vietnam</a> in May 2016 he announced the full lifting of the US arms embargo.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/barack-obama.jpg" alt="Barack Obama" width="320" height="400" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Official White House Photo by Pete Souza · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:President_Barack_Obama.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>It is sung at funerals and memorials, and in 2015 President <b>Barack Obama</b> sang it at the memorial service for Clementa Pinckney, one of the nine people killed in the Charleston church shooting.</blockquote><a href="/songs/amazing-grace">Amazing Grace</a></li><li><blockquote>Richard Vatz describes such uses as criticism of a consensus that does not examine the issues; <b>Barack Obama</b>, Mike Huckabee and Herman Cain are among the politicians who have used the phrase in this way.</blockquote><a href="/songs/kumbaya">Kumbaya</a></li><li><blockquote>Joseph Lowery began his benediction at <b>Barack Obama</b>'s inauguration on January 20, 2009, with its third stanza, and Denyce Graves sang it at the opening of the National Museum of African American History and Culture in 2016.</blockquote><a href="/songs/lift-every-voice-and-sing">Lift Every Voice and Sing</a></li><li><blockquote>Aretha Franklin sang it at <b>Barack Obama</b>'s first inauguration on 20 January 2009, and Kelly Clarkson at his second.</blockquote><a href="/songs/my-country-tis-of-thee">My Country, 'Tis of Thee</a></li><li><blockquote>…version was played as the Olympic flag entered the stadium at the opening ceremony of the 1996 Summer Olympics in Atlanta, and John Williams wrote Air and Simple Gifts for <b>Barack Obama</b>'s inauguration on 20 January 2009, played by Anthony McGill, Itzhak Perlman, Yo-Yo Ma and Gabriela Montero.</blockquote><a href="/songs/simple-gifts">Simple Gifts</a></li><li><blockquote>Before a crowd of some 1.8 million on the National Mall, <b>Barack Obama</b> was sworn in as the 44th President of the United States, taking office amid a deep financial crisis.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/january-20">20 January in history</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Kapiolani Medical Center for Women and Children, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-states">the United States</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=21.3,-157.83&amp;year=1996" aria-label="Kapiolani Medical Center for Women and Children on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="310 1376 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Barack Obama"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M760 1646h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M760 1646h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=21.3,-157.83&amp;year=1996">What was here in 1996? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
